A||N||21||Capt Fiero||2.9 and auto to 4spd swap is done||||10||
Z||000000||Capt Fiero||12-24-2000||12:05 AM||||Well I said about 4 months ago I would stop by and let everyone know how it all went.  I can say that I am happy very  happy with alot of things, the 19lb injectors that someone had me worring about on here are perfectly fine. I am going to claim, (maybe not right) that the reason that they work is that I did so much to the flow on the motor, polised the throttle body, carved out the water resritor, had a 3 angle valve grind done, the high lift cam, new valve springs, had the heads hot tanked and all the ports were cleaned up, (not really ported, just cleaned up) I am using an MSD Coil and superconductor wires.  I am upset that I did not get the tranny I wanted, (i wanted the 3.32 I ended up with the 3.65)  All in all after some tweaking on the shift cables, IAC, timming, speedo gear, trans fluid and other misc stuff, like the fact that using an auto interior harness and a standard trans harness in the engine bay came up with some interesting quirks of its own. The higher compression and bigger cam really do make a diffrence in how it sounds and the pull it has right off the line, I was really impressed at the fact that I can be in 4th gear at 1500rpms and cruise around town, I even went up a hill only slightly lugging the motor like that today just as an experement.  My total cost for the build and conversion was 1500$ CDN about 1000$ US funds because I had all of the standard to auto parts from a parts car I got for 300$ and traded for most of the machine shop work and parts that I needed. hell I even for the set of 19# injectors from ebay for 13.50$ for all eight. (that was my best deal of build) Well my web page addy should show up in the sig line but if it does not, here it is <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/capt_fiero2000/capts_page.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/capt_fiero2000/capts_page.html</A>   have fun and talk to you all later. PS if any of you are in the Vancouver BC area look me up, I am in Surrey, our next local club meeting is on the 13th of January. Just email me ahead of time.<P>------------------<BR>David<BR>85GT<BR>http://www.geocities.com/capt_fiero2000/capts_page.html||||reg||10
Z||000001||88formula||12-24-2000||12:58 AM||||Glade to see you got it done. I wish I had my engine done but my machinist is dicking me around. He is a procrastinator.<BR>Does it feel like 190 horsepower? What did you think of the simulation that I sent you made from Dyno2000? Remember I got something like 160 hp. You should put it on a real dyno after you break it in to see what kind of power you have from that engine.<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Capt Fiero||12-24-2000||01:41 AM||||Well to be very honest, I know that it is one hell of a lot faster than my old set up, but I have never really driven a notchback 4spd execpt around the block a few times.  And my car does feel stronger than that.  I had a friend test drive it for me, and he drives a ton of diffrent cars, from 5.0 rustangs to turbo all wheel drive eagle talons, and he told me that I am faster than either one of those stock. (with the execption of the brand new 13 second mustangs) I have about 300 miles on the car in the past 4 days, so I have been getting on it a bit more and more, I did run an olds cutlas 350 4 barrel today and just made him look dumb.  In the spring I will get the engien both dynoed and get some true 1/4 time slips for it.  So that I can give some real hard numbers rather than all this guessing and stuff.  I forgot to mention that I did have the exhaust manifolds proffesionlly ported out.<P>------------------<BR>David<BR>85GT<BR>http://www.geocities.com/capt_fiero2000/capts_page.html||||reg||1
Z||000003||88formula||12-24-2000||02:09 AM||||Cool! Just for the record I would like to see just how close these dyno simulations come to the real thing. I made some up for you to compare to the results you get when you put your car on a dyno and go to the track. I went to your web-site and got the camshaft specs, cool web-site by the way.  I am assuming you have ported heads and  9.5-1 compression ratio. <P>I got 170 horsepower @ 5500 rpm and 190-ft. lbs. of torque @ 4000 rpm.<P>With a 2925 lb. fiero (including driver) and a four speed transmission with the 3.65 final drive your fiero will go 0-60 in 6.56 seconds and the quarter mile in 14.67 seconds @ 95.3 mph. This is @ 800 ft. above sea level on a 75 degree day with 60% humidity. I think the part that will be the most off is the mph.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Capt Fiero||12-24-2000||12:20 PM||||I do not think that I mentioned it, but I did get my Fiero weighed, total weight of car is 2662 + 175lbs of me = 2837 so about 100lbs less than what you used, and don't forget that my wheels are 30lbs each now. MM my elevation is at damn near sea level. And 75 is dang near the hottest summer heat up here, you better drop that down to 60' for an average warm summer day. (note I race at night when the temp is about 35-40') but I will not use that.  <P>------------------<BR>David<BR>85GT<BR>http://www.geocities.com/capt_fiero2000/capts_page.html||||reg||1
Z||000005||Daleford||12-24-2000||01:16 PM||||Ahoy Capt. If I have a standard trans. car for parts, is it complicated to convert an automatic car over to standard? I hope to install a Getrag 5-speed. Merry Christmas.<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Capt Fiero||12-24-2000||01:38 PM||||The hardest part of the swap was getting the old pedal assembly out of my car and the new one in. It is easy to get out of the parts car, as it is in pieces already, but when it comes time to do yours, I had to pull the dash, center consol, guage pod, steering columm all the wiring under the dash, and all the little bits and switches and junk, WOW it was a pain. The shifter assemble requires you to pull the center consol plastic plate that runs from the ECM forward to the stereo, that is not too hard though.  Next is the hydrolic lines, that you have to run under the car and up through the spare tire panel that is another pain in the ARSE. You have to almost gut the front comparpment to get the lower panel out. Kinda like Lego Blocks, and you want the small piece in the middle so you have to take everything else around it out to get to that one block. Hell I even ended up having to remove the windshield wiper motor.  As for the wiring the only real problem that I came into was the big yellow crank wire.  On an auto there is a switch in the engine bay on the tranny that keeps the car from starting in gear, on the stick the switch is in the car on the clutch pedal.  Now I do not know if mine was a unique problem or not, but after reading the GM Service manual a few times and speaking to a friend that installs alarms and remote starts for us Fiero drivers, we I decided that my best bet was to trace the "CRANK" wire up to the bulkhead connector and make sure that I have voltage there in crank, and I did, (big yellow wire) I then cut it and put a butt connecotor on it and got some more crank wire and a few eyelet connecotors and ran the new crank wire down to the starter from the bulk head. The new wiring works perfectly and I am happy with it.  If you end up using a cradle from an 84 car, be carefull I found out the hard way that not only did GM change the wiring in 84-85 cars but the cradle does not have some of the mounts for a V6 Engine, so I had to do some drilling and changing.  Sorry for this being so long winded, but I hope that it helps a bit. Take a quick peek at my web page for some decent photos of the build.||||reg||1
Z||000007||88formula||12-24-2000||03:46 PM||||I made the adjustment for you. I changed the total weight to 2837 lbs., the temperature to 60F, and the elevation to 75 feet above sea level. <P>With those adjustments there was a big difference. I got 0-60 mph in 6.11 seconds and the quarter mile in 14.26 seconds @ 97.3 mph.<BR> When you post your dyno and track results can you either post these simulations or contact me if I miss them so I can see how close they are to the real thing?<P>I forgot to mention that the static tire diameter I used was 25 inches, the drive width I used was 7.5 inches, and the traction coefficient is 1.60. I also put in a launch rpm of 2500 and that you shift @ 6000 rpm. <P>Thanks<BR><p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 12-24-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||80's BOY||12-24-2000||06:03 PM||||how did you get those 19# injectors to work????? i have a 3.1 stroker kit in mine, alomg woth very similar mods that you have done. i bought the same injectors on e-bay for 25.00, put them in and it was so sluggish it would hardly go. kept throwing codes left and right. i didn't even leave them in for an hour. yes, i disconnected the battery before cranking it up too. crap!! why didn't it work? BTW do you have a FPR on your rail? mine's set at about 46-50 on the gauge at the fuel rail under load.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Capt Fiero||12-24-2000||06:28 PM||||I had ordered an LT1 adj. fuel pressure regulator but it did not arrive in time for me to put it in, so I went ahead and assembled the car without it.  I used the orange top injectors if that makes any diffrence. The car is by no means sluggish. I used all the existing sensors from my old 2.8 with the execption of EGR, Map, and TPS as I had a feeling that those were in bad shape, and I have a bucket of old fiero 2.8 sensors so I just dig out what I need when one goes bad. (i have had 3 complete motors with shot bottom ends that I have parted out, so I am in no short supply of sensors,plus I was given a bucket full of old 2.8 parts for free) What it boils down to is if you have the air going in, and the spark to spark all the air and fuel you should be fine.  I did my best to make sure that I was going to get the best airflow with what I had. I was on a tight budget and could not afford trick heads or anything like that. Hell my bored out throttle body is a stock one that I spent about 6 hours on and off boring out with a cylnder hone.  The biggest improvment I made to the throttle body is removing the lip on the lower half of it, the butterfly opens from the bottom in, and there is a lip about 2" toward the air intake tube that I could not see a reason for being there. So I grabbed the dremmel with some fine sanding bars and smoothed it out. I also spent a great deal of time, removing lips from the upper lower and middle intake, it is ubelievable the amount of flashing and mold lips there are on the production parts. I also took a wire wheel on the end of my drill and bored out the intake portion of the upper intake plenium (part where the throttle body meets the plenium)  Also while I was assembling everything I trimed any gasket that overlapped into any part of a port.  Oh one last thing, the exhaust system is a hollow cat, and a flowmaster muffler complete from a fiero that I got used for almost free (y pipe right to exhaust tips) Intake is drawn through a K&N filter, the water seperator box was carved out and a holley side scoop was installed.  <P>------------------<BR>David<BR>85GT<BR>http://www.geocities.com/capt_fiero2000/capts_page.html||||reg||1
Z||000010||Standard||12-25-2000||04:37 AM||||How much did you have to bore it out to get to 2.9?  That's one of the things I'm pondering doing this summer for an engine rebuild.  I was playing around with some displacment calculators and it looks like it needs a .06" bore, on an unworn engine..||||reg||1
Z||000011||88formula||12-25-2000||10:27 AM||||Just to let you know your engine won't gain a significant amount of horsepower by boring it .060" over as opposed to .020". You would gain like 1-2 horsepower more. The block would be stiffer if you leave the meat on it because the cylinder walls would be thicker. Engine cooling would also be better if you left more material on the cylinder walls. Your engine may even make more power with less of an over bore because the pistons would seal better with less flex.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Capt Fiero||12-25-2000||07:53 PM||||I went .040 over. That gave me exactly 2900 the pistons were 1mm overbore i think. The big thing was my parts guy found me some pistons with a higher compression height. So combined with the .010 deck on the heads and the slighly higher compression pistons we got more power power.  I was tossing the idea around of a 3.2 conversion using the 3.1 crank, but I looked at it like the diff between a 350 and a 327 V8, the shorter crank will give me more easy revs.  Besids I had a perfect 2.8 crank that I got from a core motor, an 87 or 88 block.  I wanted the motor to be able to rev as high as possible. SO far today I took it up to 6000 (tach said it was 6500 but I think my tach is off by 500rpms) Don't worry guys the motor already has 500km's on it.  ||||reg||1
Z||000013||Standard||12-26-2000||04:57 AM||||to clarify a little.. it looked like for an extra .1 litres of displacement, it took a .060" boring.  The 2.8 is actually a 2.845, and that got it to 2.94something.  Anyways, a 2.9 just sounds.. cool  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/cool.gif"><P>I want to build a revver, too.  Planning on some nice strong valve springs, full balance job, gasket match the intake and exhaust ports, get a bored out TB and plenum if funds allow.. Try to do everything I can to help it breath better, basically.  Not gonna mess with the compression ratio, though, 'cause I want to turbo it also  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000014||88formula||12-26-2000||05:02 PM||||Don't you think that the extra torque that an increase in crankshaft stroke provides would work better with the fieros tuned port injection system? Tuned port injection is not known for making high rpm power because of the long narrow intake runners. Besides you can rev a 3.1-3.4 to six grand just like the 2.8.<P><BR>The only replacement for displacement is turbochargers, superchargers, and nitrous when you want the most power.  <P>What do you think?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000015||Capt Fiero||12-26-2000||07:38 PM||||It is more a preferece of mine, yes you are right a 3.1-3.4 does produce more torque. But I am the guy that you see shifting at 4500rpms at 1/4 throttle. I like to long leg my shifts. When I am in town, I do spend most of my time in 4th at 1500 rpms though. But for me cruising around I like to rev the car, and a shorter strok makes for easier revs. I used the 327 - 350 as an example earlier, the 350 out powers the 327 in alot of aspects, but people still like the 327 for its so called rev happieness.  I do not know how to explain it, but at 6000rpms the 2.8 is drawing far less air in and out of the motor than a 3.4 becuase of the displacment factor. There for the fiero needs less volume in heads, and intake and exhaust to rev that high. (yes it also makes less power) but not that much less with the build that I did.   All in all it is becuase I like it and it is suited for the way I want to drive it. <P>------------------<BR>David<BR>85GT<BR>http://www.geocities.com/capt_fiero2000/capts_page.html||||reg||1
Z||000016||88formula||12-26-2000||08:11 PM||||To make your 2.9-liter engine rev even quicker you could install an aluminum flywheel. George Miller from the Fieroshop sells them. His weighs 7 lbs. and the stock one weighs more than 14 lbs.<P>Did you read the thread “need new clutch-recommendations” by fieroSTETZ? If not here it is. <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/007463.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/007463.html</A> <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000017||Capt Fiero||12-26-2000||08:41 PM||||I spoke to George from the Fiero shop, in length about what I was doing with my 2.9 and his 3.4 conversions, I also talked to him about his clutches and all of his stuff is very very cool, but I was on a tight budget on this build.  I went with a Dyno Pack clutch, and I have not yet to have any problems, it grabs so solid that I can light the tires up, and that is plenty solid for me.  It is a very short distance between fully ingaged and dissengaged. The lighter flywheel would have been nice, but again I had to think of which is more important paying the bills and eating or a possible 5% gain in power.   When money permits I am going to install either a mild turbo 5psi or a small nos kit 50-75hp shot. For the NOS I already have my MSD ignition kit picked out, window switches and all, or the turbo, I already have that worked out in my head.  A friend of a friend works at a muffler shop and can get me the y -pipe made for cheap, and I know given time I can find a turbo that will make at least 5psi for cheap. I am a bit worried about turboing a near 10-1 compression motor though. That is why I am thinking NOS.<P>------------------<BR>David<BR>85GT<BR>http://www.geocities.com/capt_fiero2000/capts_page.html||||reg||1
Z||000018||88formula||12-27-2000||05:13 PM||||NOS is what is going on my 3.4-liter engine next year. I have a 9.7-1 compression ratio and with iron heads I don't think I want to try a turbo on my motor either.||||reg||1
Z||000019||Will||12-27-2000||06:52 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Capt Fiero:<BR><B>The hardest part of the swap was getting the old pedal assembly out of my car and the new one in.<P>***<P>  Sorry for this being so long winded, but I hope that it helps a bit. Take a quick peek at my web page for some decent photos of the build.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I have done two auto to stick conversions (one an 87, one an 86, both V6's), and it was not that much trouble. You only have to remove the steering column to change the pedal brackets (which ARE something of a pain), but that is trivial since you have to change the column anyway. Removing the front bucket only requires removing the spare tire, jack, jack bracket, tire iron, windshiled washer reservoir, but nothing else. After the front bucket is out, the hydraulic lines are a snap. I certainly did not have to remove the windshiled wiper motor.<P>No wiring changes were necessary, but both of mine started and ended as V6's. The cool part is that they can now start in gear.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000020||Capt Fiero||12-27-2000||07:08 PM||||?you changed the steering columb? I used my stock columb. Did either of your cars have AC as mine did maybe that could be where I more problems than you, as well as sunroof brackets and such.  ||||reg||1
Z||000021||Will||12-28-2000||09:49 AM||||Both cars were fully optioned except for the factory sub.<BR>I changed the steering column because the P-N interlock (keeps the key from turning unless the shifter is in P or N) runs a pull cable from the shifter up through the dash to the steering column. I didn't feel like figuring out how to defeat this and make it work without the cable at all, so I just changed to the stickshift column, which was easy since I already had the auto column out.<BR>||||reg||1
